<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">University of Tübingen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1477">1477</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Eberhard Karls University of Tübingen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a> was founded in 1477 by Count Eberhard V of Württemberg, called the Bearded, in his town of Tübingen. It became the university of the Duchy of Württemberg and, after the duchy adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in 1534, a centre of Lutheran theology. Its Protestant seminary, the Tübinger Stift, trained clergy and scholars, among them Hegel, Hölderlin and Schelling.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/university-of-tubingen.jpg" alt="University of Tübingen" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Prissantenbär · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uni_T%C3%BCbingen_Neue_Aula_Sommer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Count Eberhard V, called the Bearded, later the first Duke of Württemberg as Eberhard I, founded the university in 1477. He was a civic and ecclesiastical reformer who set up the school after becoming absorbed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> revival of learning during his travels to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Its first rector was Johannes Nauclerus.</p><p>During a plague outbreak in 1482 the staff and students took refuge in a series of towns, Dornstetten, Rottenburg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-urach">Urach</a> and finally Waiblingen, and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a> only in 1483. Duke Carl Eugen gave the university its present name in 1769 by adding his own first name to that of the founder. It later became the principal university of the Kingdom of Württemberg.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The university has a long history of innovative thought, particularly in theology, in which it and the Tübinger Stift are known. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon">Philip Melanchthon</a>, a chief figure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation</a>, helped establish its direction. Hölderlin, Hegel and Schelling, called the Tübingen Three, were roommates at the Stift, and the astronomer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-kepler">Johannes Kepler</a> was among its students.</p><p>In the middle of the 19th century the Protestant theologian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-christian-baur">Ferdinand Christian Baur</a> and his circle, the Tübingen School, pioneered the historical-critical analysis of biblical and early Christian texts. The university founded the first faculty of economics and business in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1817 and the first faculty of natural sciences in 1863, and Friedrich Miescher discovered DNA there in 1868.</p><p>In the Nazi period the university played a leading role in efforts to present the policies of the Third Reich as scientific. It dismissed the fewest Jewish employees of any German university in 1933, because it had hardly any Jewish scholars or students. The physicist Hans Bethe was dismissed on 20 April 1933 as non-Aryan, and at least 1,158 people were sterilised at the university hospital. There was no significant resistance. Theologian Helmut Thielicke helped revive the university after the war from 1947. In 1966 Joseph Ratzinger, later Pope Benedict XVI, took a chair in dogmatic theology.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The university is not a campus university but is spread through the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a>, in buildings of the old town, the Wilhelmstraße area, the Morgenstelle science campus built in the 1970s and the teaching hospitals. Schloss Hohentübingen houses the collections of the Museum of the University of Tübingen, among them mammoth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> figures and bone flutes from the Vogelherdhöhle, among the oldest figurative works of art and musical instruments. It is the only university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> with a faculty of rhetoric, founded by Walter Jens. Eleven Nobel laureates are associated with it, among them Eduard Buchner, Hans Bethe and Christiane Nüsslein-Volhard.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “University of Tübingen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>University of Tübingen</b> and its Protestant seminary trained clergy, officials and scholars, and the theologian Ferdinand Christian Baur and his pupil David Friedrich Strauss, whose Life of Jesus of 1835 caused a sensation, made the Tübingen school central to modern biblical criticism.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wurttemberg">Kingdom of Württemberg · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Count Eberhard V the Bearded reunited it by the Treaty of Münsingen in 1482 and founded the <b>University of Tübingen</b> in 1477.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-wurttemberg">County of Württemberg</a></li><li><blockquote>The County of Württemberg, reunited by Count Eberhard im Bart in 1482, was raised to a duchy at the Diet of Worms in 1495; Eberhard had founded the <b>University of Tübingen</b> in 1477.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-wurttemberg">Duchy of Württemberg</a></li></ul></section>
